*.sh text eol=lf
Dockerfile text eol=lf
*.yml text eol=lf
*.yaml text eol=lf

# Mutation anchors are byte-exact strings, and 234 of the 754 in
# test/mutations/*.json span a newline. Without an eol rule a Windows checkout
# under the default core.autocrlf=true rewrites those LF bytes to CRLF, the
# anchors stop matching, and spec-static-check reports a zero-hit anchor rather
# than a platform difference. Measured on git 2.55.0.windows.3: deleting 3 .js
# files and forcing a re-checkout with core.autocrlf=true already broke 48
# anchors; a full re-checkout broke 205. The rules above covered only
# .sh/.yml/.yaml, so the JavaScript tree was exposed.
#
# text=auto is deliberately NOT set: it would let git guess per file and
# reintroduce the mixed state. These are explicit, and `eol=lf` fixes the
# working-tree form independently of what core.autocrlf says.
*.js text eol=lf
*.mjs text eol=lf
*.cjs text eol=lf
*.json text eol=lf
*.md text eol=lf
*.html text eol=lf

# Binary assets must never be line-ending converted. Some are already matched
# by extension, but the packaged exe and the image fixtures have none.
*.exe binary
*.png binary
*.jpg binary
*.jpeg binary
*.ico binary
*.zip binary
*.gz binary
